We would like the prove the following result:

$$
\dfrac{\textrm d}{\textrm d x}(\tanh{x}) = \sech^2{x}
$$

We do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is

$$
\tanh{x} = \dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.
$$

Differentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at

$$
\begin{aligned}\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}x}(\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & =\frac{\text{d}}{\text{d}x}(\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}) \\  & =\frac{({e}^{2x}-1)^{′}\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)^{′}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\  & =\frac{(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)(2{e}^{2x})}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\  & =\frac{2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\  & =\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\  & ={(\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1})}^{2} \\  & ={(\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}})}^{2} \\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x.\end{aligned}
$$

A sector of a circle of radius $3\,\textrm{cm}$ and its corresponding central angle measuring

$$
\theta=\dfrac{\pi}{3}
$$

radians are shown below. How can we calculate the area of the circular sector?

![](images/c2/c26f3663862de465d8cd5adaf9f7cfa166088b8142a83eb29dbe5b4c97a1292f.png)

Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the area of the sector. To find a formula for $\mathcal{A}$, the trick is to set up a proportion.

The ratio of the sector area ($\mathcal{A}$) to the total area ($\pi r^2$) must be equal to the ratio of the angle ($\theta$) to a full revolution ($2\pi$). So, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{A}{πr^{2}} &= \frac{θ}{2π} \\
πr^{2} \cdot \frac{A}{πr^{2}} &= πr^{2} \cdot \frac{θ}{2π} \\
A &= \frac{r^{2}θ}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

In our example, we have $r=3$ and

$$
\theta= \dfrac{\pi}{3}
$$

so we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
A &= \frac{3^{2} \cdot \frac{π}{3}}{2} = \frac{3π}{2}cm^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

**Note:** The formula

$$
\mathcal A = \dfrac{r^2 \theta}{2}
$$

is very important, and we will use it throughout this lesson and beyond. You should aim to remember it. It's also important to note that the angle $\theta$ must be in radians.

To add two numbers given in scientific notation with the same power of ten, we simply add the decimal parts.

To demonstrate, let's add the numbers $7.7\times 10^4$ and $1.2 \times 10^4$.

Since both numbers are multiplied by $10^4$, we add the decimal parts and then multiply the result by $10^4$. By doing this, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
(7.7 \cdot 10^{4}) + (1.2 \cdot 10^{4}) &= (7.7 + 1.2) \cdot 10^{4} \\
&= 8.9 \cdot 10^{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

